 begin with Arthur C. Clarke's Rama, where we explore the grand architecture of our solar system starting with simple models.","Building on that mechanical view, an orrery specifically predicts how planets move relative to each other in space.","From there we see that these motions follow curved paths called orbits, driven by gravity pulling objects toward a center.","Stepping up a level, research now treats planets not as points but as spinning shapes with complex internal dynamics.","Finally, this advanced theory suggests the motion of orbiting bodies might even power the Sun's magnetic activity and sunspots."],"clips":["/demo/bridge/r_8660f9/0.mp3","/demo/bridge/r_8660f9/1.mp3","/demo/bridge/r_8660f9/2.mp3","/demo/bridge/r_8660f9/3.mp3","/demo/bridge/r_8660f9/4.mp3"]}